<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Bamberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1245 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Bamberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Franconian church state founded by Emperor Henry II, whose cathedral holds the Bamberg Horseman and whose bishops staged notorious witch trials.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry II</a> founded the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in 1007 and endowed it richly; he and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunigunde-of-luxembourg">Kunigunde</a> are buried in its cathedral. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gathered lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a>, and after the extinction of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Andechs-Meranien</a> in 1248 they consolidated a territory and ruled it as imperial princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter elected the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. The Romanesque-Gothic cathedral, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg-horseman">Bamberg Horseman</a> and the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-ii">Pope Clement II</a>, symbolises the see's standing. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-georg-ii-fuchs-von-dornheim">Johann Georg II Fuchs von Dornheim</a> the territory suffered one of the worst witch persecutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between 1626 and 1631. In the baroque era <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothar-franz-von-schonborn">Lothar Franz von Schönborn</a> built the New Residence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The prince-bishopric was secularised in 1802 and annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, ending more than eight centuries of episcopal rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> became an archdiocese in 1817, and its largely intact old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side">